Thanks. I've run a few preliminary experiments on a few of the data
sets. Both the regular and active SVMs do very well on landscapes but
poorly
on all of the other topics (barely getting above 50% test set accuracy
for
detecting architecture pictures). I tried it on normalized and
unnormalized verions of the data. It could well be that SVMs aren't
suited to these particular topics however it's surprising that they
should
really do so badly. I wonder if you could run a sanity check on the
feature values? I'll also check my SVM and data processing code as well.
Do you use "edges" as a feature? It would seem that for buildings and
man-made objects such as buldings the "edginess" or, even better, the
"cornerness" of an image may be useful. It appears that the current
feature set may not be enough to allow one to effectively discriminate
between buildings, people and birds.

I've attached the current SVM code although it probably will be
changing. It currently only works with standard .dat file rather then
our special multi-labeled dat files. Instructions for the regular SVM
learner are on Thorsten's web page:
http://www-ai.cs.uni-dortmund.de/SOFTWARE/SVM_LIGHT/svm_light.eng.html

I've added a few more parameters, mainly for active learning.


Here are the parameters that I use:

* For passive inductive learning:

svm_learn -z 0 -b 0 -t 1 -d <poly_degree>
desired_topic.train.passive.dat desired_topic.passive.mdl

desired_topic.train.passive.dat is a .dat file containing labeled
training data. The program outputs a model file called
desired_topic.passive.mdl. <poly_degree> is the degree of the polynomial
kernels (a degree of 1 corresponds to learning hyperplanes).

* For Simple active inductive learning:

svm_learn -z 0 -b 0 -o 1 -t 1 -d <poly_degree> -k
desired_topic.train.allLabels.dat -n <num_queries>
desired_topic.train.active.dat desired_topic.simpleactive.mdl

desired_topic.train.active.dat is a large .dat file containing all
(unlabeled) data instances in the pool. All of the unlabeled data
instances should be labeled with a 0. I also include one positive and
one negative labeled instance in desired_topic.train.active.dat as well.

desired_topic.train.allLabels.dat is a regular .dat file that is
identical to desired_topic.train.active.dat except that it contains all
of the labels of the data instances. This it the file the svm_learn
looks at when it queries the label of a pool instance.

svm_learn will perform <num_queries> queries and then output a model to
desired_topic.simpleactive.mdl.


* To classify unseen test data using a model file:

svm_classify desired_topic.test.dat desired_topic.simpleactive.mdl
desired_topic.simpleactive.out

where desired_topic.test.dat is a fully labeled test set.

* A few more notes:

I've noticed that svm_light is not very numerically stable so it
sometimes does not converge.

The MaxRatio and MaxMargin methods probably are going to be far too slow
with a large database -- probably best to stick with the Simple active
learning method.

As I mentioned before, my modification of Thorsten's code really wasn't
written or designed for outside use so please don't distribute the code
to other people.

Thanks,

Simon.

